  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and ARBs (angiotensin receptor blockers) are both recommended as equal first-line options for treating high blood pressure (hypertension), but generally offer a better safety and tolerability profile.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "generally offer a better safety and tolerability profile"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "How They Work"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "ACE inhibitors (drugs ending in -pril, like lisinopril) stop the body from making angiotensin II, a hormone that narrows blood vessels ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "ARBs (drugs ending in -sartan, like losartan) block angiotensin II from attaching to the receptor sites on blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Both classes relax blood vessels to lower blood pressure, protect the kidneys, and help the heart ."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Effectiveness vs. Safety"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Effectiveness: Large studies show that ACE inhibitors and ARBs lower blood pressure and prevent major heart events (like heart attacks or strokes) equally well."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects: ACE inhibitors often cause a nagging dry cough and carry a small risk of angioedema (dangerous deep tissue swelling) because they increase a substance called bradykinin. ARBs do not block bradykinin breakdown, meaning they rarely cause a cough and have a much lower risk of angioedema."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Discontinuation: Because of fewer side effects, patients are less likely to stop taking ARBs compared to ACE inhibitors."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Guidelines"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Traditional guidelines historically placed ACE inhibitors as the initial choice due to older trial data, while using ARBs as an alternative if the ACE inhibitor caused a cough."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Newer large-scale real-world data and expert consensus increasingly favor starting with an ARB first to avoid the common side effects of ACE inhibitors while achieving the same blood pressure control."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Note: Neither class should ever be used during pregnancy, and they should not be taken together at the same time."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Are you or a family member currently taking one of these medications?"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Are you experiencing any side effects like a cough?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can provide more tailored information or discuss alternative options with you."
      }
    ],
